message:
I am thinking...: So HUFF_BC is 20. The `bit_length` array has 20 elements. The bug is in the inner loop:

```c
for(k = 0; k < value + 2; k++) {
    bit_length[w++] = 0;
}
```

The outer loop checks `w < HUFF_BC` (i.e., w < 20) only at the top of each iteration. But inside, when doing RLE expansion, it can write beyond the array bounds because there's no check that `w < HUFF_BC` inside the inner fill loop.

For example, if w = 19 (one slot left) and value = 15 (i.e., ESCAPE followed by 15), then value + 2 = 17, so it would try to write 17 zeros starting from index 19, overflowing the 20-element array by 16 bytes.

Wait, let me re-read the code more carefully:

```c
if(value == ESCAPE) {  // value == 15
    value = (p[i] & nibble_mask) >> nibble_shift;  // read next nibble
    ...
    if(value == 0) {
        bit_length[w++] = ESCAPE;  // literal 15
    } else {
        int k;
        for(k = 0; k < value + 2; k++) {
            bit_length[w++] = 0;  // fill value+2 zeros
        }
    }
}
```

So when we encounter ESCAPE (15) followed by a non-zero nibble value V, we fill V+2 zeros. If V can be 1-15 (since it's a nibble), the maximum fill is 17 zeros.

If w is, say, 14 when we enter the inner loop with V=15, we'd write 17 zeros starting at index 14, going up to index 30 - which is 11 bytes past the end of the 20-element array. The crash says "WRITE 7" which matches a similar overflow.

Now I need to craft a RAR5 file that triggers this. Let me understand the RAR5 format structure to get to the `parse_tables` function.

Let me look at how RAR5 files are structured and how we get to `parse_tables`.

